WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.425
At Allen, electrical work is for qualified people

00:07.425 --> 00:09.912
only. If you're not one, that doesn't mean you

00:09.912 --> 00:12.000
have nothing to do with electricity.

00:12.000 --> 00:15.125
It means your job is different. You look for problems.

00:15.125 --> 00:17.350
You report them. You lock out the equipment you're

00:17.350 --> 00:20.263
working on, using your own lock. And you never

00:20.263 --> 00:24.663
repair, reset, bypass or open up electrical equipment,

00:24.663 --> 00:26.113
even when it looks easy.

00:27.241 --> 00:33.253
The standards back this up. Under 30 CFR 57.12021,

00:33.253 --> 00:36.003
danger signs are posted at all major electrical

00:36.003 --> 00:41.378
installations. Under 57.12022, those areas are

00:41.378 --> 00:43.965
entered only by authorized persons.

00:43.965 --> 00:48.653
And under 57.12020, dry wooden platforms,

00:48.653 --> 00:52.015
insulating mats or other nonconductive material

00:52.015 --> 00:54.578
are kept in place at switchboards and power-control

00:54.578 --> 00:56.978
switches where shock hazards exist.

00:56.978 --> 00:59.816
Inspectors cited that mat standard four times at

00:59.816 --> 01:03.078
Boonesboro from 2021 through our latest data.

01:03.078 --> 01:05.403
If you see a missing mat, report it.

01:05.403 --> 01:07.865
Don't move one, and don't stand on a bare floor

01:07.865 --> 01:09.191
to work a switch.

01:12.527 --> 01:14.840
Here's the first just-reset-it moment.

01:14.840 --> 01:17.302
A conveyor stops, and someone says the breaker

01:17.302 --> 01:19.927
tripped again, just go flip it back on.

01:19.927 --> 01:22.427
If you're not qualified and authorized to operate

01:22.427 --> 01:25.889
it, the answer is no. A breaker that trips is protecting

01:25.889 --> 01:28.889
something. Resetting it over and over can feed

01:28.889 --> 01:32.489
power into a damaged motor or cable until it fails,

01:32.489 --> 01:35.577
burns or shocks someone. Report it and wait for

01:35.577 --> 01:36.977
a qualified person.

01:38.104 --> 01:41.342
Second moment: a fuse is blown, and there's a spare

01:41.342 --> 01:47.092
on the shelf. Under 57.12036, fuses aren't removed

01:47.092 --> 01:50.054
or replaced by hand in an energized circuit,

01:50.054 --> 01:53.267
and not at all in an energized circuit unless equipment

01:53.267 --> 01:56.392
and techniques designed to prevent shock are used.

01:56.392 --> 01:58.966
Putting in a bigger fuse to stop the nuisance is

01:58.966 --> 02:02.454
even worse. It removes the protection completely.

02:02.454 --> 02:04.579
This is a qualified person's job.

02:05.736 --> 02:09.273
Third: you find a panel door open with wires showing,

02:09.273 --> 02:11.586
or a cover plate off a junction box.

02:11.586 --> 02:14.298
Don't reach in to push wires back, and don't stand

02:14.298 --> 02:16.848
in front of it to close it. Keep people away and

02:16.848 --> 02:22.436
report it. Under 57.12030, when a potentially dangerous

02:22.436 --> 02:25.673
condition is found, it's corrected before the equipment

02:25.673 --> 02:29.498
or wiring is energized. From 2021 through our latest

02:29.498 --> 02:32.498
data, inspectors cited electrical protection,

02:32.498 --> 02:35.611
controls, conductors and uncorrected dangerous

02:35.611 --> 02:38.261
conditions six times at Dix River.

02:39.438 --> 02:41.576
Fourth: you need a machine running,

02:41.576 --> 02:44.113
and someone else's lock is on its disconnect.

02:44.113 --> 02:51.076
Never remove it. Under 56 and 57.12016 and 12017,

02:51.076 --> 02:53.638
locks and warning devices are removed only by the

02:53.638 --> 02:57.038
person who put them on or by authorized personnel.

02:57.038 --> 03:00.001
Find your supervisor. If the owner has gone home,

03:00.001 --> 03:02.826
authorized personnel follow the mine's procedure,

03:02.826 --> 03:05.101
starting with making sure that person really is

03:05.101 --> 03:07.838
clear of the equipment. A lock you remove without

03:07.838 --> 03:10.751
that check could be the one keeping someone alive.

03:11.941 --> 03:14.466
Fifth: you pick up a portable tool in the shop

03:14.466 --> 03:16.978
and the cord is cut, or the ground pin has been

03:16.978 --> 03:19.866
snapped off the plug so it fits an old outlet.

03:19.866 --> 03:22.266
Don't use it, and don't fix it with tape.

03:22.266 --> 03:24.578
Unplug it if you can do that safely,

03:24.578 --> 03:26.953
tag it out of service the way your mine requires,

03:26.953 --> 03:29.553
and report it. A missing ground pin takes away

03:29.553 --> 03:31.778
the path that would trip the protection if the

03:31.778 --> 03:34.978
tool shorts. The next person to use it may be standing

03:34.978 --> 03:36.191
on a wet floor.

03:37.325 --> 03:40.475
Sixth: a motor won't start, so someone keeps pressing

03:40.475 --> 03:43.000
the start button, or holds it in. If it doesn't

03:43.000 --> 03:45.412
start the normal way, something is wrong.

03:45.412 --> 03:48.487
It might be jammed, overloaded, single-phasing

03:48.487 --> 03:50.900
or locked out by somebody you can't see.

03:50.900 --> 03:53.700
Stop pressing. If you smell burning insulation

03:53.700 --> 03:56.912
or see smoke, keep people back, get the power shut

03:56.912 --> 03:59.112
off by the people authorized to do it,

03:59.112 --> 04:00.575
and report it right away.

04:03.903 --> 04:06.502
So here's your part. Look for damaged cords,

04:06.502 --> 04:09.815
cables, boxes and switches, missing mats and open

04:09.815 --> 04:13.028
panels. Report them right away. Keep people clear

04:13.028 --> 04:15.877
until a qualified person arrives. Lock out the

04:15.877 --> 04:18.415
equipment you're working on with your own lock.

04:18.415 --> 04:22.228
And never reset, bypass or repair electrical equipment.

04:22.228 --> 04:25.252
That isn't being unhelpful. It's knowing your lane,

04:25.252 --> 04:27.152
and it keeps everyone alive.

04:28.268 --> 04:31.430
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

04:31.430 --> 04:32.930
override this video.
